reasoning:
Let's look at the fromISO in interval.js. The bug: when Settings.throwOnInvalid is true, `Interval.invalid` throws. But the issue: `Duration.fromISO` may throw an InvalidDurationError when throwOnInvalid is set... Actually the expected behavior for `Interval.fromISO works with Settings.throwOnInvalid` — let's look at the actual luxon source (from memory) — in luxon's interval.js fromISO:

```js
  static fromISO(text, opts) {
    const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
    if (s && e) {
      let start, startIsValid;
      try {
        start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts);
        startIsValid = start.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        startIsValid = false;
      }

      let end, endIsValid;
      try {
        end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);
        endIsValid = end.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        endIsValid = false;
      }

      if (startIsValid && endIsValid) {
        return Interval.fromDateTimes(start, end);
      }

      if (startIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(e, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.after(start, dur);
        }
      } else if (endIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(s, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.before(end, dur);
        }
      }
    }
    return Interval.invalid("unparsable", `the input "${text}" can't be parsed asISO 8601`);
  }
```

Yes, that's the fix. The bug is that when throwOnInvalid is set, DateTime.fromISO throws instead of returning invalid, so Interval.fromISO throws instead of returning invalid interval / throwing InvalidIntervalError.

